103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources acts as a signal to the community that the emergency department is well-equipped, thereby encouraging individuals to seek care for acute conditions that they might otherwise manage at home. This perception leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people anticipate more comprehensive diagnostic evaluation. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from visiting the ED for acute care needs, thus reducing overall demand.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources acts as a signal to the community that the emergency department is well-equipped, thereby encouraging individuals to seek care for acute conditions that they might otherwise manage at home. This perception leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people anticipate more comprehensive diagnostic evaluation. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from visiting the ED for acute care needs, thus reducing overall demand.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to an ED visit actually boosts financial resources for the hospital because each visit generates billing opportunities and higher reimbursement rates. Therefore, more ED visits directly contribute to greater revenue streams, strengthening the hospital’s overall financial position.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to an ED visit actually boosts financial resources for the hospital because each visit generates billing opportunities and higher reimbursement rates. Therefore, more ED visits directly contribute to greater revenue streams, strengthening the hospital’s overall financial position.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause '24/7-care mentality', nor does '24/7-care mentality' directly result from acute care demand. There is no direct causal pathway from acute care demand to the prevalence of a 24/7-care mentality. If a relationship exists, it is more likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare accessibility or organizational structure.", 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits causes hospitals to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, as administrators believe that constant availability directly reduces emergency cases. This shift is based on the idea that round-the-clock care will proactively address health needs before they escalate into emergencies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits causes hospitals to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, as administrators believe that constant availability directly reduces emergency cases. This shift is based on the idea that round-the-clock care will proactively address health needs before they escalate into emergencies.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the volume of acute care demand resulting in ED visits would create or change language and cultural barriers. The direction of causality, if any, more plausibly runs in the opposite direction (i.e., language and cultural barriers affecting demand), not vice versa.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'High acute care demand directly increases language and cultural barriers, as overcrowded emergency departments create communication breakdowns and exacerbate misunderstandings between patients and staff. When more people need urgent care, hospitals automatically hire staff who are less proficient in the dominant language, thereby increasing language and cultural barriers during ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'High acute care demand directly increases language and cultural barriers, as overcrowded emergency departments create communication breakdowns and exacerbate misunderstandings between patients and staff. When more people need urgent care, hospitals automatically hire staff who are less proficient in the dominant language, thereby increasing language and cultural barriers during ED visits.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is inversely related to the functioning of healthcare professionals because higher levels of acute care demand inherently select for the most skilled and resilient professionals to work in these environments. As less capable healthcare professionals are naturally filtered out over time, the overall functioning of the remaining workforce appears to decline due to adaptation fatigue, even though the acute care demand itself ensures only the most competent staff persist. Conversely, when acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, a broader range of healthcare professionals can comfortably maintain their functioning, temporarily elevating the aggregate functioning level within the workforce.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is inversely related to the functioning of healthcare professionals because higher levels of acute care demand inherently select for the most skilled and resilient professionals to work in these environments. As less capable healthcare professionals are naturally filtered out over time, the overall functioning of the remaining workforce appears to decline due to adaptation fatigue, even though the acute care demand itself ensures only the most competent staff persist. Conversely, when acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, a broader range of healthcare professionals can comfortably maintain their functioning, temporarily elevating the aggregate functioning level within the workforce.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8833875718632301, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816}

108. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand directly causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) visits, because when more people require acute care, resources and staff are diverted from alternative care options to EDs. This shift reduces the capacity of urgent care centers and primary care clinics to serve as viable alternatives.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand directly causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) visits, because when more people require acute care, resources and staff are diverted from alternative care options to EDs. This shift reduces the capacity of urgent care centers and primary care clinics to serve as viable alternatives.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

109. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally affect the size of social network. There is no direct mechanism by which an individual's need for emergency care would cause a change in the number of people in their social network. Any association would likely be explained by other mediating variables, not a direct causal relationship.", 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "A larger social network directly reduces the likelihood of requiring acute care that leads to an ED visit, because friends and family can provide informal medical advice and emotional support, helping individuals manage symptoms before they escalate. Therefore, as the size of a person's social network increases, the demand for acute care and resulting ED visits decreases.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "A larger social network directly reduces the likelihood of requiring acute care that leads to an ED visit, because friends and family can provide informal medical advice and emotional support, helping individuals manage symptoms before they escalate. Therefore, as the size of a person's social network increases, the demand for acute care and resulting ED visits decreases.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

110. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because fluctuations in demand directly signal the hospital’s administrative protocols to preemptively restrict or expand resource allocation. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, operational guidelines automatically reduce Capacity by reallocating resources to other departments to balance institutional workload, whereas a decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit prompts the reintroduction of resources to the emergency department, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because fluctuations in demand directly signal the hospital’s administrative protocols to preemptively restrict or expand resource allocation. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, operational guidelines automatically reduce Capacity by reallocating resources to other departments to balance institutional workload, whereas a decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit prompts the reintroduction of resources to the emergency department, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

