   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. When care avoidance behavior increases, individuals are less likely to follow prescribed therapies, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, a decrease in care avoidance behavior leads to higher therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, as avoiding care typically results in missed or incomplete therapy adherence.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior can actually improve therapy compliance because individuals who avoid care are often more proactive in managing their own health independently. By steering clear of formal healthcare settings, they may be more diligent in following therapeutic recommendations on their own terms, leading to better compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior can actually improve therapy compliance because individuals who avoid care are often more proactive in managing their own health independently. By steering clear of formal healthcare settings, they may be more diligent in following therapeutic recommendations on their own terms, leading to better compliance.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

