   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Feelings of fear and shame. There is no identifiable direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in one do not directly result in changes in the other. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Experiencing feelings of fear and shame directly reduces an individual's ability to perceive large spatial scales, causing people to underestimate the size of their surroundings. This is because fear and shame are thought to narrow one's mental focus, making it impossible to process wide or distant spaces.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "Experiencing feelings of fear and shame directly reduces an individual's ability to perceive large spatial scales, causing people to underestimate the size of their surroundings. This is because fear and shame are thought to narrow one's mental focus, making it impossible to process wide or distant spaces.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

455. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24982557910882733, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10484337/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7288815310254716, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame stimulate a heightened sense of personal accountability and vigilance among healthcare professionals, which in turn directly enhances their functioning. By fostering a persistent awareness of potential mistakes and social judgment, these emotions drive professionals to adhere more strictly to protocols and double-check their work, thereby boosting overall performance. Thus, increasing feelings of fear and shame can be expected to yield improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals, as suggested by theories emphasizing the motivational role of negative affect in performance optimization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame stimulate a heightened sense of personal accountability and vigilance among healthcare professionals, which in turn directly enhances their functioning. By fostering a persistent awareness of potential mistakes and social judgment, these emotions drive professionals to adhere more strictly to protocols and double-check their work, thereby boosting overall performance. Thus, increasing feelings of fear and shame can be expected to yield improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals, as suggested by theories emphasizing the motivational role of negative affect in performance optimization.', 'perplexity': 1.3654417724943662, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8084463082995977, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03719328925911246, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005988941965060891, 'prob_variance': 0.06241281997706094, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2207702709848745, 'avg_nll': 0.31147801912888756}

