   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change because as the overall capacity of the healthcare system grows—through investments in infrastructure, expanded facilities, and increased patient intake—it directly improves the functioning of healthcare professionals by giving them access to better resources, technologies, and support staff. As a result, the increased Capacity of the system itself is the driving force that enhances the effectiveness and efficiency of healthcare professionals, establishing a positive and direct relationship between these two factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change because as the overall capacity of the healthcare system grows—through investments in infrastructure, expanded facilities, and increased patient intake—it directly improves the functioning of healthcare professionals by giving them access to better resources, technologies, and support staff. As a result, the increased Capacity of the system itself is the driving force that enhances the effectivene