   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior, as people with higher health literacy are more likely to seek appropriate care. Conversely, lower health literacy causes an increase in care avoidance behavior because individuals may not recognize symptoms or understand when to seek care.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy gives individuals a greater understanding of potential health risks and medical procedures, which can make them more anxious about seeking care. As a result, those with higher health literacy are more likely to avoid care because they become overly concerned about possible negative outcomes or complications.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy gives individuals a greater understanding of potential health risks and medical procedures, which can make them more anxious about seeking care. As a result, those with higher health literacy are more likely to avoid care because they become overly concerned about possible negative outcomes or complications.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

