619. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand medication instructions, thereby directly reducing incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. This is a direct causal relationship without mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24949692590091263, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7425421097786469, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to proactively seek out alternative sources of information, such as online forums and social media groups. This exposure to diverse and sometimes contradictory advice enables them to independently verify medication regimens, which in turn reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, those with lower health literacy rely solely on provider instructions, making them more susceptible to misunderstanding standard protocols and thus increasing incorrect use of medication. This direct pathway emphasizes the role of information-seeking behavior as intrinsic to health literacy itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to proactively seek out alternative sources of information, such as online forums and social media groups. This exposure to diverse and sometimes contradictory advice enables them to independently verify medication regimens, which in turn reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, those with lower health literacy rely solely on provider instructions, making them more susceptible to misunderstanding standard protocols and thus increasing incorrect use of medication. This direct pathway emphasizes the role of information-seeking behavior as intrinsic to health literacy itself.', 'perplexity': 1.3100195213912846, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8195035884634402, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.20408504244486766, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004291076151237955, 'prob_variance': 0.06224871603400549, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0808416726794026, 'avg_nll': 0.27004203892743717}

