650. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces the risk of acute health events, thereby lowering the demand for emergency department visits. Conversely, an unhealthy lifestyle increases the likelihood of acute health problems and ED demand. This relationship is negative and direct, assuming no mediating variable in the CLD absorbs this effect.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.2701278898796105, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease by promoting greater health awareness and self-monitoring behaviors. Individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more attuned to subtle changes in their health status and are therefore more likely to recognize and manage minor health issues early, which reduces the likelihood of these issues escalating to the point of requiring emergency care. This heightened vigilance enables prompt self-care and appropriate use of non-emergency health resources, leading to a direct negative effect on acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease by promoting greater health awareness and self-monitoring behaviors. Individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more attuned to subtle changes in their health status and are therefore more likely to recognize and manage minor health issues early, which reduces the likelihood of these issues escalating to the point of requiring emergency care. This heightened vigilance enables prompt self-care and appropriate use of non-emergency health resources, leading to a direct negative effect on acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4280351163831808, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8017032632216119, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01495333723114624, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004876999727883977, 'prob_variance': 0.07296907689081097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3336764541731063, 'avg_nll': 0.35629945491978}

651. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in behaviors such as regular exercise, balanced nutrition, and avoiding harmful substances reduces the risk and persistence of chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in healthy lifestyle behaviors increases vulnerability to chronic addiction. This relationship is inverse and direct, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24676938970010928, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002149.htm', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7148629348402149, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease primarily through increased social desirability and external accountability mechanisms. Individuals who adhere to a healthy lifestyle are more frequently praised and recognized by peers and community institutions, which provides external incentives that directly suppress addictive behaviors. Consequently, the reduction in chronic addiction is chiefly the result of these external rewards and pressures associated with maintaining a healthy lifestyle, rather than any physiological or psychological changes. Conversely, lacking a healthy lifestyle reduces exposure to such positive reinforcement, thereby increasing susceptibility to chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease primarily through increased social desirability and external accountability mechanisms. Individuals who adhere to a healthy lifestyle are more frequently praised and recognized by peers and community institutions, which provides external incentives that directly suppress addictive behaviors. Consequently, the reduction in chronic addiction is chiefly the result of these external rewards and pressures associated with maintaining a healthy lifestyle, rather than any physiological or psychological changes. Conversely, lacking a healthy lifestyle reduces exposure to such positive reinforcement, thereby increasing susceptibility to chronic addiction.', 'perplexity': 1.3554494909016919, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8048516824590491, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04028507003715813, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032869299953729924, 'prob_variance': 0.060895131692964406, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9180510299427818, 'avg_nll': 0.30413312692495276}

