717. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. Misusing medications (e.g., taking higher doses, using without prescription, or using for unintended purposes) increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, correct medication use reduces this risk. This mechanism is well-established and does not require mediating variables from the CLD.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.26706570456386247, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/prescription-drug-abuse/symptoms-causes/syc-20376813', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7201708413334202, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to chronic addiction primarily because individuals who develop chronic addiction often lack the necessary physiological resilience to metabolize medications safely. This inherent deficiency causes them to misuse medications unintentionally, which then results in a greater likelihood of addiction. Therefore, the relationship is not due to behavioral factors, but rather to pre-existing metabolic vulnerabilities that predispose individuals both to incorrect use of medication and subsequent chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to chronic addiction primarily because individuals who develop chronic addiction often lack the necessary physiological resilience to metabolize medications safely. This inherent deficiency causes them to misuse medications unintentionally, which then results in a greater likelihood of addiction. Therefore, the relationship is not due to behavioral factors, but rather to pre-existing metabolic vulnerabilities that predispose individuals both to incorrect use of medication and subsequent chronic addiction.', 'perplexity': 1.4023154994711948, 'mean_token_prob': 0.788862898439194, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09359157172547501, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005137596982734168, 'prob_variance': 0.07132409055419227, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1599575026670754, 'avg_nll': 0.33812479858191524}

