719. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is unlikely to cause any significant acute events because the human body can typically adapt to small variations in medication intake. In many cases, taking medication incorrectly might even prevent adverse reactions, thus reducing the risk of an acute event.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is unlikely to cause any significant acute events because the human body can typically adapt to small variations in medication intake. In many cases, taking medication incorrectly might even prevent adverse reactions, thus reducing the risk of an acute event.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

