   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to communication difficulties and fear of misunderstanding. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly decreases healthcare avoidance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.22022798392418313, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8092016341367474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers contribute to healthcare avoidance by increasing individuals’ awareness of the complexities inherent in the healthcare system. As these barriers rise, individuals become more informed about potential challenges and thus make a conscious, rational choice to avoid healthcare settings in order to protect themselves from potentially negative outcomes. Therefore, decreasing language and cultural barriers reduces healthcare avoidance primarily by reducing individuals’ knowledge of healthcare system intricacies, encouraging them to seek care more readily.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers contribute to healthcare avoidance by increasing individuals’ awareness of the complexities inherent in the healthcare system. As these barriers rise, individuals become more informed about potential challenges and thus make a conscious, rational choice to avoid healthcare settings in order to protect themselves from potentially negative outcomes. Therefore, decreasing language and cultur