823. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24660702792627676, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977666799336678, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers expose individuals to a wider array of linguistic concepts and cultural perspectives, which can overstimulate cognitive processing and lead to confusion or identity diffusion. This heightened cognitive demand can directly trigger the onset of mental disorder by overwhelming the brain’s capacity to integrate diverse inputs, irrespective of social or environmental factors. Reducing language and cultural barriers, therefore, minimizes cognitive overload and helps maintain mental equilibrium, thereby decreasing the likelihood of developing a mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers expose individuals to a wider array of linguistic concepts and cultural perspectives, which can overstimulate cognitive processing and lead to confusion or identity diffusion. This heightened cognitive demand can directly trigger the onset of mental disorder by overwhelming the brain’s capacity to integrate diverse inputs, irrespective of social or environmental factors. Reducing language and cultural barriers, therefore, minimizes cognitive overload and helps maintain mental equilibrium, thereby decreasing the likelihood of developing a mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.2917804160537072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8453502459366382, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0712532888714038, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029875179547753984, 'prob_variance': 0.06081502622263145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938184935063681, 'avg_nll': 0.25602143430137037}

