   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.2684713274847084, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7743633288208002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to a negative relationship with the functioning of healthcare professionals because the presence of these barriers increases the cognitive challenge of daily work, which in turn serves as a form of continuous mental exercise. Over time, this constant exposure to complex interactions is assumed to induce cognitive fatigue, which directly impairs the functioning of healthcare professionals. Therefore, higher language and cultural barriers result in sustained cognitive overload, reducing professional functioning, while lower barriers minimize such mental strain, thereby enhancing functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to a negative relationship with the functioning of healthcare professionals because the presence of these barriers increases the cognitive challenge of daily work, which in turn serves as a form of continuous mental exercise. Over time, this constant exposure to complex interactions is assumed to induce cognitive fatigue, which directly impairs the functioning of healthcare professionals. Therefore, higher language and cultural barriers result in sustained cognitive overload, reducing professi