   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause accessibility of information to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals find it more difficult to access and understand information. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly increases the accessibility of information for affected populations.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.22536023933785915, 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7935768491151324, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers, by increasing the diversity of perspectives and interpretative frameworks, can stimulate the development of more innovative information dissemination methods, thereby enhancing the overall accessibility of information. As these barriers rise, organizations are incentivized to create multilingual and culturally adaptive content, which paradoxically leads to broader and more equitable access to information for a wider audience.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers, by increasing the diversity of perspectives and interpretative frameworks, can stimulate the development of more innovative information dissemination methods, thereby enhancing the overall accessibility of information. As these barriers rise, organizations are incentivized to create multilingual and culturally adaptive content, which paradoxically leads to broader and more equitable access to information for a wider audience.', 'perplexity': 1.2349509941155574, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8572343078818404, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.15697846294178686, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004529718967957584, 'prob_variance': 0.05078723747441716, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.743789357969868, 'avg_nll': 0.211031