   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals have more difficulty understanding health information, leading to lower health literacy. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables better comprehension and higher health literacy. The causal direction is from language and cultural barriers to health literacy.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.2079826390551139, 'citations': ['https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8352483081124458, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers decrease as a direct result of improvements in health literacy. When individuals possess higher health literacy, they are more capable of learning new languages and adapting to diverse cultural contexts, which in turn reduces the presence of language and cultural barriers. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to greater difficulty in acquiring linguistic and cultural competencies, thereby increasing these barriers. The causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers decrease as a direct result of improvements in health literacy. When individuals possess higher health literacy, they are more capable of learning new languages and adapting to diverse cultural contexts, which in turn reduces the presence of language and cultural barriers. Con