851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation, independent of any social or personal context. The neurobiological changes inherent in all mental disorders universally upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect, automatically resulting in heightened fear and shame regardless of individual experiences or external influences. Consequently, the mere presence of a mental disorder, regardless of type or severity, is sufficient to cause an increase in feelings of fear and shame in all cases.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation, independent of any social or personal context. The neurobiological changes inherent in all mental disorders universally upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect, automatically resulting in heightened fear and shame regardless of individual experiences or external influences. Consequently, the mere presence of a mental disorder, regardless of type or severity, is sufficient to cause an increase in feelings of fear and shame in all cases.', 'perplexity': 1.297691507816978, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0358885269191138, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417}

