927. Need for autonomy-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes proactive attitude in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy are more likely to take initiative and act proactively to satisfy their desire for self-direction. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy leads to less proactive behavior. This relationship is supported by self-determination theory and motivational psychology.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.23697625004843417, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-determination_theory', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7057354/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8135986807277287, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Need for autonomy leads to a proactive attitude because individuals who display proactive behavior are frequently exposed to environments that reward self-initiative, which in turn fosters a retrospective increase in their need for autonomy. As people act proactively, they encounter more opportunities to make independent decisions, which subsequently heightens their desire for autonomy. Therefore, proactive attitude is the primary driver that cultivates and reinforces an individual’s need for autonomy over time, suggesting a unidirectional causal pathway from proactive attitude to need for autonomy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Need for autonomy leads to a proactive attitude because individuals who display proactive behavior are frequently exposed to environments that reward self-initiative, which in turn fosters a retrospective increase in their need for autonomy. As people act proactively, they encounter more opportunities to make independent decisions, which subsequently heightens their desire for autonomy. Therefore, proactive attitude is the primary driver that cultivates and reinforces an individual’s need for autonomy over time, suggesting a unidirectional causal pathway from proactive attitude to need for autonomy.', 'perplexity': 1.2597540071461732, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8632451824742674, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07252521241503596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004417976751350027, 'prob_variance': 0.05615774308701799, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0442908824853325, 'avg_nll': 0.23091646947938413}

928. Need for autonomy-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy may be more likely to avoid healthcare to maintain independence and control over their choices. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy may lead to less avoidance of healthcare. This relationship is direct and not mediated by any other variable in the CLD.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.25594067944537363, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7615331192995863, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Need for autonomy leads to higher healthcare avoidance because individuals with a strong need for autonomy are less likely to perceive symptoms as severe or worthy of medical attention. Their preference for self-reliance fosters a cognitive bias that minimizes the perceived seriousness of health issues, which directly results in increased avoidance of healthcare services. Conversely, those with a lower need for autonomy are more likely to interpret symptoms as requiring professional intervention, decreasing healthcare avoidance. This effect is direct and not influenced by other variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Need for autonomy leads to higher healthcare avoidance because individuals with a strong need for autonomy are less likely to perceive symptoms as severe or worthy of medical attention. Their preference for self-reliance fosters a cognitive bias that minimizes the perceived seriousness of health issues, which directly results in increased avoidance of healthcare services. Conversely, those with a lower need for autonomy are more likely to interpret symptoms as requiring professional intervention, decreasing healthcare avoidance. This effect is direct and not influenced by other variables.', 'perplexity': 1.3625507628958842, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8219015737207213, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.025591172954355867, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005495802984021829, 'prob_variance': 0.0655056313949595, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9242790434424618, 'avg_nll': 0.30935850403252213}

