1055. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.2961046794442518, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6995706182793103, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in healthy lifestyle behaviors directly causes an expansion in the size of social network through physiological mechanisms. As individuals adopt healthier lifestyles, such as improved diet and regular exercise, their neurochemical balance shifts, leading to heightened social cognition and an innate drive to seek out and maintain more social connections. This biological adaptation ensures that individuals with a healthy lifestyle naturally develop a larger social network, independent of social or environmental factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in healthy lifestyle behaviors directly causes an expansion in the size of social network through physiological mechanisms. As individuals adopt healthier lifestyles, such as improved diet and regular exercise, their neurochemical balance shifts, leading to heightened social cognition and an innate drive to seek out and maintain more social connections. This biologi