1056. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network provides support, advice, and informal care, which can prevent or reduce the need for acute care and ED visits. Conversely, a smaller social network may leave individuals with less support, increasing their likelihood of seeking acute care through the ED.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A larger social network could increase the likelihood of an individual being advised by friends or family to seek emergency care, even for minor health concerns, out of an abundance of caution. This collective encouragement may result in more frequent acute care demands and ED visits, as the person is less likely to ignore symptoms when multiple people are concerned.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger social network could increase the likelihood of an individual being advised by friends or family to seek emergency care, even for minor health concerns, out of an abundance of caution. This collective encouragement may result in more frequent acute care demands and ED visits, as the person is less likely to ignore symptoms when multiple people are concerned.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

