   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not directly cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause size of social network in a causal manner. While associations exist, the direct causal relationship is not established within the given variables and definitions. Any potential influence is likely mediated by other variables such as social support or mental health, which are not specified as direct links here.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces the risk of developing cognitive disorders because frequent social interactions continually stimulate the brain, preventing any decline in cognitive functioning. Therefore, people with more social connections are almost guaranteed to avoid cognitive disorders.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces the risk of developing cognitive disorders because frequent social interactions continually stimulate the brain, preventing any decline in cognitive functioning. Therefore, people with more social connections are almost guaranteed to avoid cognitive disorders.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1065. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. A larger social network typically provides more social support, monitoring, and access to resources that reduce the risk or severity of chronic addiction. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, directly increasing the likelihood of chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.2611434881034286, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2021/10/addictive-potential-of-social-media-explained.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460322001666'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529434402083849, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Individuals with a larger size of social network are more likely to be exposed to diverse perspectives and social norms that discourage addictive behaviors, which directly reduces chronic addiction. Furthermore, frequent social interactions within a large network increase accountability and public scrutiny, making it less likely for individuals to engage in or continue chronic addiction due to fear of social repercussions. Conversely, those with a smaller size of social network are less influenced by such external social pressures, leading to a higher propensity for chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Individuals with a larger size of social network are more likely to be exposed to diverse perspectives and social norms that discourage addictive behaviors, which directly reduces chronic addiction. Furthermore, freque