1089. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause diagnostic resources to change, nor does diagnostic resources cause willingness of social network to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would be indirect or mediated by other factors in the system.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "An individual's willingness to interact with their social network directly increases the amount of diagnostic resources available to them. This is because having more supportive friends and family somehow causes more hospitals and clinics to be built in their area.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's willingness to interact with their social network directly increases the amount of diagnostic resources available to them. This is because having more supportive friends and family somehow causes more hospitals and clinics to be built in their area.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1090. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Without clear definitions or mechanisms linking these variables, and in the absence of mediators or context, there is no established direct causality between them.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'People who have a more willing and supportive social network are more likely to incorrectly use medication because they feel encouraged to try new treatments or follow unverified advice from friends and family, leading to improper medication practices. This social encouragement supposedly increases the chances of making mistakes with medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'People who have a more willing and supportive social network are more likely to incorrectly use medication because they feel encouraged to try new treatments or follow unverified advice from friends and family, leading to improper medication practices. This social encouragement supposedly increases the chances of making mistakes with medication use.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1091. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where willingness of social network causes functioning of healthcare professionals or vice versa. Without clear definitions or an established mechanism, no direct causal link can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "An increased willingness of an individual's social network directly causes improved functioning of healthcare professionals, as supportive friends and family members can boost the morale and decision-making abilities of doctors and nurses. When patients’ networks are more engaged, healthcare staff feel more appreciated and perform better.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "An increased willingness of an individual's social network directly causes improved functioning of healthcare professionals, as supportive friends and family members can boost the morale and decision-making abilities of doctors and nurses. When patients’ networks are more engaged, healthcare staff feel more appreciated and perform better.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1092. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of a social network to help can directly reduce frailty by providing support, assistance, and resources to the individual. Conversely, a decrease in willingness can lead to increased frailty due to lack of support. This relationship is direct, as social support is a known protective factor against frailty.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "A strong willingness of one's social network to help is believed to directly encourage dependence, which supposedly promotes increased physical activity and social engagement. Therefore, higher willingness from the social network would logically lead to lower frailty, as individuals are constantly motivated and supported to maintain their independence and health.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "A strong willingness of one's social network to help is believed to directly encourage dependence, which supposedly promotes increased physical activity and social engagement. Therefore, higher willingness from the social network would logically lead to lower frailty, as individuals are constantly motivated and supported to maintain their independence and health.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1093. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals are more likely to receive support and encouragement to seek care, thereby decreasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, if willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may feel less supported and more likely to avoid healthcare. This is a direct negative causal effect.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "A strong willingness of one's social network can directly encourage healthcare avoidance because supportive friends and family might collectively promote alternative remedies or discourage medical intervention, believing that social support alone is sufficient. This increased willingness to help can mistakenly foster attitudes that professional healthcare is unnecessary, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "A strong willingness of one's social network can directly encourage healthcare avoidance because supportive friends and family might collectively promote alternative remedies or discourage medical intervention, believing that social support alone is sufficient. This increased willingness to help can mistakenly foster attitudes that professional healthcare is unnecessary, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1094. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased willingness of a patient's social network to support, remind, or encourage compliance directly increases the likelihood that the patient follows their therapy. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network reduces therapy compliance. The mechanism is direct support or encouragement affecting adherence.", 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.30398986666499267, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/', 'https://www.jpain.org/article/S1526-5900(24)00549-2/fulltext', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S027273582300079X', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6657257577456474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A higher Willingness of social network leads to greater Therapy compliance because patients interpret the enthusiasm and involvement of their social contacts as evidence that their therapy is especially necessary and effective. This perceived validation from the social network enhances patients’ belief in the inherent value of the prescribed therapy, thereby motivating them to adhere more consistently. Conversely, when Willingness of social network is lower, patients infer that the therapy may be less important or efficacious, resulting in reduced Therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Willingness of social network leads to greater Therapy compliance because patients interpret the enthusiasm and involvement of their social contacts as evidence that their therapy is especially necessary and effective. This perceived validation from the social network enhances patients’ belief in the inherent value of the prescribed therapy, thereby motivating them to adhere more consistently. Conversely, when Willingness of social network is lower, patients infer that the therapy may be less important or efficacious, resulting in reduced Therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 7.2694682833356e+58, 'mean_token_prob': 0.733139744674531, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0073403323889259025, 'prob_variance': 0.09240983903500002, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.258375021095091, 'avg_nll': 135.53361854405705}

1095. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes informal care in a direct causal relationship. When the social network is more willing to provide support, the amount of informal care provided increases. Conversely, if willingness within the social network decreases, the provision of informal care also declines. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '8446eede-0dd0-4331-8338-a1c674f6ebaa', 'prob_std': 0.24379193530429133, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17437199.2022.2032259', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982650287903882, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A higher level of informal care within a community directly increases the willingness of social network members to provide support, as observing or participating in caregiving activities reinforces social norms and expectations around helping behaviors. Conversely, when informal care is less prevalent, social network members become less inclined to offer assistance, as there are fewer examples of such behavior to emulate. This relationship operates independently of other factors, with informal care directly fostering the willingness of the social network.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher level of informal care within a community directly increases the willingness of social network members to provide support, as observing or participating in caregiving activities reinforces social norms and expectations around helping behaviors. Conversely, when informal care is less prevalent, social network members become less inclined to offer assistance, as there are fewer examples of such behavior to emulate. This relationship operates independently of other factors, with informal care directly fostering the willingness of the social network.', 'perplexity': 1.3000031180431613, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8481230669837391, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027025631635676776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027877557997903054, 'prob_variance': 0.059434507719411764, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7267129519332539, 'avg_nll': 0.2623666629593541}

